WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:01.730
- The McDonald's coffee cup case,

00:00:01.730 --> 00:00:05.150
the case where the aunt sued
her nephew for hugging her,

00:00:05.150 --> 00:00:08.080
these are all examples
of the most ridiculous,

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:09.390
frivolous lawsuits ever,

00:00:09.390 --> 00:00:11.140
(air whooshes)
or are they?

00:00:11.140 --> 00:00:13.310
One of the hallmarks of the
American legal system is that

00:00:13.310 --> 00:00:14.940
if you've been injured, you have the right

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:17.530
to have your grievance
heard in a court of law.

00:00:17.530 --> 00:00:19.714
Of course, one of the downsides
of the American legal system

00:00:19.714 --> 00:00:21.610
is that if you haven't been injured,

00:00:21.610 --> 00:00:23.800
you also have the right to
have your grievance heard

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:27.500
in a court of law, but not
every newsroom or TV show

00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:29.080
has a legal consultant on hand,

00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:32.070
so they often leave out
key details and nuances

00:00:32.070 --> 00:00:33.200
about particular cases.

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:35.980
And they have a hard time
determining which ones are which,

00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:37.660
and occasionally these
sensational headlines

00:00:37.660 --> 00:00:39.220
that focus only on the dollar amounts

00:00:39.220 --> 00:00:40.960
can sometimes give people
the wrong impression

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:42.290
about the merits of the case.

00:00:42.290 --> 00:00:43.450
So today we're gonna discuss

00:00:43.450 --> 00:00:45.390
some of the most frivolous lawsuits

00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:48.385
that actually might not have
been so frivolous after all.

00:00:48.385 --> 00:00:50.968
(bright music)

00:00:52.094 --> 00:00:53.630
And, of course, if we're
discussing lawsuits

00:00:53.630 --> 00:00:55.180
that have been labeled as frivolous,

00:00:55.180 --> 00:00:57.660
but actually might have a
kernel of truth to them,

00:00:57.660 --> 00:00:59.190
there is no better place to start

00:00:59.190 --> 00:01:01.660
than the err frivolous lawsuit.

00:01:01.660 --> 00:01:04.550
That is, of course, the
McDonald's coffee cup lawsuit.

00:01:04.550 --> 00:01:05.930
So let's start this discussion

00:01:05.930 --> 00:01:07.750
with the story of Stella Liebeck,

00:01:07.750 --> 00:01:10.200
the woman who spilled
scolding hot McDonald's coffee

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:12.370
on her lap and sued the restaurant.

00:01:12.370 --> 00:01:13.690
Now, here are the facts,

00:01:13.690 --> 00:01:15.950
as you might not have
ever heard them before.

00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:18.930
On February 27th, 1992, Stella Liebeck,

00:01:18.930 --> 00:01:22.140
then a 79 year old woman
from Albuquerque, New Mexico,

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:23.670
ordered a cup of McDonald's coffee

00:01:23.670 --> 00:01:24.780
at the drive-through window.

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:27.860
Liebeck was sitting in the
passenger seat of her parked car

00:01:27.860 --> 00:01:30.930
as her grandson stepped inside
to grab some cream and sugar.

00:01:30.930 --> 00:01:32.390
Since the car had no cup holders,

00:01:32.390 --> 00:01:33.690
Liebeck placed the coffee cup

00:01:33.690 --> 00:01:36.260
between her knees and
pulled to open the lid.

00:01:36.260 --> 00:01:39.310
She then spilled the entire
cup of scalding hot coffee

00:01:39.310 --> 00:01:42.130
on her lap, causing third
degree burns on her thighs,

00:01:42.130 --> 00:01:43.030
buttocks and groin.

00:01:43.030 --> 00:01:45.670
- All I remember is trying
to get out of the car.

00:01:45.670 --> 00:01:48.870
I screamed, not realizing
I was burned that bad.

00:01:48.870 --> 00:01:50.820
I knew I was in terrible pain.

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:52.530
- She was actually
hospitalized for eight days

00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:53.830
and underwent skin grafting.

00:01:53.830 --> 00:01:55.990
Liebeck was permanently
disfigured after the incident

00:01:55.990 --> 00:01:57.840
and was partially disabled for two years.

00:01:57.840 --> 00:01:59.880
Some of the trial exhibits that were used

00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:01.390
in this particular case,

00:02:01.390 --> 00:02:04.230
shine the light on just how
devastating her injuries were.

00:02:04.230 --> 00:02:05.810
Now, of course, Liebeck sued McDonald's,

00:02:05.810 --> 00:02:07.360
accusing the company of gross negligence

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:08.193
and for selling coffee,

00:02:08.193 --> 00:02:10.020
that was, quote, unreasonably dangerous

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:11.720
and defectively manufactured.

00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:12.790
At trial, it was uncovered

00:02:12.790 --> 00:02:14.410
that McDonald's had been serving coffee

00:02:14.410 --> 00:02:16.950
between 180 and 190 degrees Fahrenheit,

00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:18.660
much higher than the industry norm

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:20.240
and which expert testimony revealed

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:23.490
could cause third degree burns
in as little as two seconds.

00:02:23.490 --> 00:02:26.240
Other internal documents
obtained from McDonald's showed

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:28.620
that between 1982 and 1992,

00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:31.410
the company had received more
than 700 reports of people

00:02:31.410 --> 00:02:32.900
being burned by their coffee

00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:35.480
with McDonald's having settled
claims from said injuries

00:02:35.480 --> 00:02:37.820
for more than $500,000.

00:02:37.820 --> 00:02:40.400
In this case, the jury
awarded Liebeck $200,000

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:41.740
in compensatory damages,

00:02:41.740 --> 00:02:45.540
but reduced that by 20%
to $160,000 to account

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:47.870
for her own negligence
in spilling on herself.

00:02:47.870 --> 00:02:50.500
The jury also awarded her $2.7 million

00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:51.820
based on her attorney's suggestion

00:02:51.820 --> 00:02:55.050
to penalize McDonald's for two
days worth of coffee revenue,

00:02:55.050 --> 00:02:57.630
or about $1.35 million per day.

00:02:57.630 --> 00:03:00.020
The punitive damage awards
was by the discovery

00:03:00.020 --> 00:03:02.600
that 700 people had been
previously burned by hot coffee,

00:03:02.600 --> 00:03:04.660
demonstrating that the
company had actual knowledge

00:03:04.660 --> 00:03:06.480
that the coffee was being served too hot,

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:07.710
but failed to remedy the issue.

00:03:07.710 --> 00:03:09.510
The judge reduced the punitive damages

00:03:09.510 --> 00:03:12.840
to three times compensatory
damages, or 480,000,

00:03:12.840 --> 00:03:16.810
for a total judgment of $640,000.

00:03:16.810 --> 00:03:19.270
This decision was appealed,
but the parties settled

00:03:19.270 --> 00:03:22.930
for an undisclosed amount of
probably less than $600,000.

00:03:22.930 --> 00:03:24.470
Now, if prior to watching this video,

00:03:24.470 --> 00:03:25.570
you thought that this was a case

00:03:25.570 --> 00:03:28.180
about a clumsy, greedy woman,
trying to get rich quick

00:03:28.180 --> 00:03:31.070
over her own mistake,
you're probably not alone.

00:03:31.070 --> 00:03:33.930
Thanks to a perfect storm
of short news reports

00:03:33.930 --> 00:03:35.380
and outraged political conservatives

00:03:35.380 --> 00:03:37.500
who use the case to
advocate for sharp limits

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:40.270
on tort lawsuits, so-called tort reform,

00:03:40.270 --> 00:03:42.550
the general public was really misinformed

00:03:42.550 --> 00:03:44.420
about the nature of this case.

00:03:44.420 --> 00:03:45.960
- [Commentator] She spilled
hot coffee on her lap

00:03:45.960 --> 00:03:48.530
while sitting in our car
and claimed it was too hot.

00:03:48.530 --> 00:03:49.820
- [Narrator] Every day we hear about

00:03:49.820 --> 00:03:51.810
another outrageous lawsuit.

00:03:51.810 --> 00:03:54.500
- As a result, the general
public wrongly believed

00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:56.770
Liebeck spilled coffee while
she was driving her car.

00:03:56.770 --> 00:03:58.850
She was, in fact, just a
passenger in a parked car,

00:03:58.850 --> 00:03:59.980
and that the injuries were minor.

00:03:59.980 --> 00:04:01.700
She was, in fact, permanently disfigured.

00:04:01.700 --> 00:04:03.330
Late night comedians in sitcoms

00:04:03.330 --> 00:04:05.120
have also heavily parodied the case,

00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:08.200
contributing to the false image
of this particular lawsuit.

00:04:08.200 --> 00:04:10.180
- You get me one coffee
drinker on that jury,

00:04:10.180 --> 00:04:11.621
you're gonna walk out of there a rich man.

00:04:11.621 --> 00:04:13.130
(audience laughs)

00:04:13.130 --> 00:04:14.410
- And despite what you might've heard,

00:04:14.410 --> 00:04:17.180
Liebeck did not Sue McDonald's
for millions of dollars.

00:04:17.180 --> 00:04:18.910
In fact, she didn't
want to sue them at all.

00:04:18.910 --> 00:04:20.940
She originally requested only $20,000

00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:23.130
to cover her medical
bills and lost income.

00:04:23.130 --> 00:04:25.087
McDonald's responded with an insulting

00:04:25.087 --> 00:04:26.900
$800 settlement offer.

00:04:26.900 --> 00:04:29.150
Liebeck's counsel had also
offered settlement amounts

00:04:29.150 --> 00:04:31.610
ranging from 90,000 to $300,000,

00:04:31.610 --> 00:04:34.410
but McDonald's also rejected
all pre-trial efforts

00:04:34.410 --> 00:04:35.490
to settle the case.

00:04:35.490 --> 00:04:37.550
- I was not in it for the money.

00:04:37.550 --> 00:04:39.190
I was in it, because I want them

00:04:39.190 --> 00:04:40.960
to bring the temperature down,

00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:42.730
so that other people will not go through

00:04:42.730 --> 00:04:43.820
the same thing I did.

00:04:43.820 --> 00:04:46.650
- The most that Liebeck asked
for at trial was $800,000,

00:04:46.650 --> 00:04:49.840
and it was the jury that
awarded her $2.7 million,

00:04:49.840 --> 00:04:51.320
which she never actually received,

00:04:51.320 --> 00:04:53.380
because the punitive damages were chopped.

00:04:53.380 --> 00:04:54.213
And her critics claim

00:04:54.213 --> 00:04:55.570
that Liebeck shouldn't recover anything,

00:04:55.570 --> 00:04:57.110
because she spilled coffee on herself.

00:04:57.110 --> 00:04:58.670
But the jury did, in fact,

00:04:58.670 --> 00:05:01.260
find Liebeck partially
responsible for her injury,

00:05:01.260 --> 00:05:03.650
applying the principles
of comparative negligence

00:05:03.650 --> 00:05:05.170
in which liability is a portion

00:05:05.170 --> 00:05:07.850
based on how much it fault
each party was for the injury.

00:05:07.850 --> 00:05:11.030
The jury found that Beck was
20% at fault for her injury,

00:05:11.030 --> 00:05:14.330
and her compensatory
damages were reduced by 20%,

00:05:14.330 --> 00:05:17.050
the amount that she was at
fault, but in the past decade,

00:05:17.050 --> 00:05:18.990
the Liebeck case has
received renewed attention

00:05:18.990 --> 00:05:21.810
in the form of the 2011
documentary "Hot coffee"

00:05:21.810 --> 00:05:25.400
and a 2013 retro report video
from the New York Times.

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:27.710
But the false perception
about this case continues

00:05:27.710 --> 00:05:28.620
to this very day.

00:05:28.620 --> 00:05:32.370
- That Stella Liebeck needed
to defend a reputation

00:05:32.370 --> 00:05:35.190
is the saddest piece of this whole story.

00:05:35.190 --> 00:05:36.030
- But now what do you think,

00:05:36.030 --> 00:05:38.230
completely frivolous or
is there some merit to it?

00:05:38.230 --> 00:05:41.010
Let's move on to the next
case about the famous man

00:05:41.010 --> 00:05:43.350
who was trapped in a phone
booth and hit by a drunk driver,

00:05:43.350 --> 00:05:45.080
but sues the phone company.

00:05:45.080 --> 00:05:47.430
As you might recall, the
1980s were a unique period

00:05:47.430 --> 00:05:49.364
in our history when people
wore parachute pants,

00:05:49.364 --> 00:05:51.550
and MTV actually played music.

00:05:51.550 --> 00:05:53.470
That was also when the
tort reform movement,

00:05:53.470 --> 00:05:55.160
which claimed America was under assault

00:05:55.160 --> 00:05:57.730
by unscrupulous lawyers,
bringing frivolous lawsuits

00:05:57.730 --> 00:05:59.290
first began to pick up steam.

00:05:59.290 --> 00:06:01.300
And few were as instrumental
in cementing this image

00:06:01.300 --> 00:06:03.930
of out of control lawsuits
into the American consciousness

00:06:03.930 --> 00:06:05.310
than president Ronald Reagan,

00:06:05.310 --> 00:06:08.230
but more on that in a
moment, because in 1974,

00:06:08.230 --> 00:06:11.210
Charles Bigbee following a
night shift as a custodian

00:06:11.210 --> 00:06:14.410
in the city of Los Angeles
used a public telephone booth

00:06:14.410 --> 00:06:15.630
to call his girlfriend.

00:06:15.630 --> 00:06:16.540
As the two spoke,

00:06:16.540 --> 00:06:18.780
Bigbee saw a car driving
erratically towards him,

00:06:18.780 --> 00:06:20.700
but while bystanders and a man

00:06:20.700 --> 00:06:22.530
in a neighboring phone booth were able

00:06:22.530 --> 00:06:24.040
to get out of the way,

00:06:24.040 --> 00:06:25.130
Bigbee's phone booth jammed,

00:06:25.130 --> 00:06:27.760
trapping him inside for
the near fatal collusion.

00:06:27.760 --> 00:06:30.150
And this is how president
Reagan described that case

00:06:30.150 --> 00:06:32.440
in a speech to the American
Tort Reform Association.

00:06:32.440 --> 00:06:35.360
- Now it's no surprise
that the injured man sued,

00:06:35.360 --> 00:06:38.423
but you might be startled
to hear whom he sued,

00:06:39.280 --> 00:06:43.290
the telephone company
and associated firms.

00:06:43.290 --> 00:06:44.270
That's right.

00:06:44.270 --> 00:06:46.050
- Reagan claimed that this loony case

00:06:46.050 --> 00:06:47.700
was an example of a broken tort system.

00:06:47.700 --> 00:06:49.030
Sounds pretty bad, right?

00:06:49.030 --> 00:06:50.090
Well, it might surprise you to know

00:06:50.090 --> 00:06:52.960
that Reagan left out a few
key details about this case,

00:06:52.960 --> 00:06:54.970
which would not be the first time.

00:06:54.970 --> 00:06:57.130
- A few months ago, I
told the American people

00:06:57.130 --> 00:07:00.000
I did not trade arms for hostages.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:03.710
My heart and my best intention
still tell me that's true,

00:07:03.710 --> 00:07:06.940
but the facts and the
evidence tell me it is not.

00:07:06.940 --> 00:07:09.150
- So here's what president
Reagan actually left out.

00:07:09.150 --> 00:07:12.420
First, two years prior, a driver
had destroyed a phone booth

00:07:12.420 --> 00:07:14.420
that had been placed
in the identical spot.

00:07:14.420 --> 00:07:15.950
Now the phone company replaced the booth,

00:07:15.950 --> 00:07:18.250
but didn't include a
guard rail or a warning,

00:07:18.250 --> 00:07:21.180
and the replacement phone booth
had a malfunctioning door.

00:07:21.180 --> 00:07:23.170
Accordingly, the phone
company was on notice

00:07:23.170 --> 00:07:25.200
that the placement of the
phone booth might be dangerous,

00:07:25.200 --> 00:07:28.340
and didn't take what most would
consider reasonable measures

00:07:28.340 --> 00:07:29.370
to remedy the risk.

00:07:29.370 --> 00:07:30.940
And contrary to Reagan's implication,

00:07:30.940 --> 00:07:33.130
Bigbee did not merely
sue the phone company.

00:07:33.130 --> 00:07:35.770
Bigbee sued everyone he felt
had contributed to his accident

00:07:35.770 --> 00:07:37.250
including the alleged drunk driver,

00:07:37.250 --> 00:07:39.160
as well as the horse racing track

00:07:39.160 --> 00:07:41.360
he believed served the
alcohol to the driver.

00:07:41.360 --> 00:07:42.800
And this is typical of lawsuits

00:07:42.800 --> 00:07:44.310
where you name all of the parties

00:07:44.310 --> 00:07:45.800
that you believe are responsible

00:07:45.800 --> 00:07:48.610
and let the facts take
the jury where they will.

00:07:48.610 --> 00:07:50.770
And the jury can apportion faults

00:07:50.770 --> 00:07:52.520
among all of the different defendants.

00:07:52.520 --> 00:07:54.530
And, finally, Reagan left out

00:07:54.530 --> 00:07:56.450
just how badly Bigbee was injured.

00:07:56.450 --> 00:07:58.580
Bigbee had to have his right leg amputated

00:07:58.580 --> 00:08:00.110
four inches above the knee.

00:08:00.110 --> 00:08:03.000
And while his left leg required
skin grafts to be saved,

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:04.870
Bigbee would eventually
require an artificial leg,

00:08:04.870 --> 00:08:07.180
a wheelchair, and a knee
brace for his left leg.

00:08:07.180 --> 00:08:09.410
And because insurance
would not cover everything,

00:08:09.410 --> 00:08:12.760
Bigbee's unpaid medical bills
amounted to more than $1,500,

00:08:12.760 --> 00:08:16.480
20% of his yearly janitor salary of 7,300.

00:08:16.480 --> 00:08:17.770
Bigbee was physically unable to work

00:08:17.770 --> 00:08:19.250
and became severely depressed

00:08:19.250 --> 00:08:21.110
and was hounded by collections agents.

00:08:21.110 --> 00:08:23.200
But in the end, frivolous or not,

00:08:23.200 --> 00:08:25.970
Bigbee settled for a mere $25,000

00:08:25.970 --> 00:08:28.130
with the alleged drunk driver paying half

00:08:28.130 --> 00:08:30.030
and the racetrack vendors
paying the other half.

00:08:30.030 --> 00:08:31.780
But since this is America,
and we love our cars

00:08:31.780 --> 00:08:33.400
almost as much as we love our guns,

00:08:33.400 --> 00:08:35.860
let's talk about the supposedly
frivolous case of the man

00:08:35.860 --> 00:08:39.330
who won $1.25 million
after stealing his own car.

00:08:39.330 --> 00:08:41.500
This is a case that you
just read the headlines,

00:08:41.500 --> 00:08:43.100
and it seems completely insane.

00:08:43.100 --> 00:08:47.000
Man steals his own car
and wins $1.25 million.

00:08:47.000 --> 00:08:48.910
Well, reading below the headline reveals

00:08:48.910 --> 00:08:51.920
a harrowing story of discrimination
and police brutality.

00:08:51.920 --> 00:08:54.010
In 2015, Dr. Lawrence Crosby,

00:08:54.010 --> 00:08:56.510
then a 25 year old doctoral student,

00:08:56.510 --> 00:08:58.870
was fixing a loose molding on his car.

00:08:58.870 --> 00:09:00.130
A woman witnessed Crosby,

00:09:00.130 --> 00:09:02.320
who was wearing a hooded
sweatshirt at the time,

00:09:02.320 --> 00:09:03.590
fixing his car and assumed

00:09:03.590 --> 00:09:05.130
that he was trying to steal the car.

00:09:05.130 --> 00:09:08.050
She proceeded to follow him
in her car from his home

00:09:08.050 --> 00:09:10.340
to the university before she
called them police to report

00:09:10.340 --> 00:09:12.830
that she saw a black man
in a hoodie steal a car.

00:09:12.830 --> 00:09:14.970
In the arrest video, Crosby
can be heard telling the police

00:09:14.970 --> 00:09:16.500
that he was trying to fix his own car.

00:09:16.500 --> 00:09:18.320
The police dash cam video shows Crosby

00:09:18.320 --> 00:09:19.890
getting out of his car with both hands up,

00:09:19.890 --> 00:09:21.210
holding a cell phone in one hand.

00:09:21.210 --> 00:09:23.560
Officers approached Crosby
with their guns drawn.

00:09:23.560 --> 00:09:25.420
Police then ordered Crosby
to get down on the ground.

00:09:25.420 --> 00:09:27.180
And when he did not immediately comply,

00:09:27.180 --> 00:09:28.810
a group of officers rushed Crosby

00:09:28.810 --> 00:09:30.090
and threw him to the ground.

00:09:30.090 --> 00:09:32.040
Crosby said that the
officers piled on top of him,

00:09:32.040 --> 00:09:33.500
proceeded to hit and knee him.

00:09:33.500 --> 00:09:35.730
And as an officer screamed
to stop resisting,

00:09:35.730 --> 00:09:38.627
Crosby, who had his own dash
cam video, can be heard saying,

00:09:38.627 --> 00:09:41.140
"I'm cooperating, Sir, you're on video."

00:09:41.140 --> 00:09:42.100
That's an FYI.

00:09:42.100 --> 00:09:44.060
Officer's quickly learned
that Crosby owned the car

00:09:44.060 --> 00:09:45.110
and was in good standing.

00:09:45.110 --> 00:09:47.170
And even though Crosby had
not actually stolen a car

00:09:47.170 --> 00:09:48.003
that belonged to him,

00:09:48.003 --> 00:09:49.560
Crosby was still arrested and charged

00:09:49.560 --> 00:09:52.280
with disobeying officers
and resisting police.

00:09:52.280 --> 00:09:54.920
To repeat, even though Crosby
was cleared by the police

00:09:54.920 --> 00:09:56.090
for stealing his own car,

00:09:56.090 --> 00:09:58.330
the police still arrested
him for resisting arrest

00:09:58.330 --> 00:10:00.420
for a crime that he, in
fact, did not commit.

00:10:00.420 --> 00:10:02.030
A judge later throughout the charges,

00:10:02.030 --> 00:10:04.270
and the following year,
Crosby filed a civil lawsuit

00:10:04.270 --> 00:10:06.290
against the city of Evanston, Illinois,

00:10:06.290 --> 00:10:08.340
citing false arrest and excessive force.

00:10:08.340 --> 00:10:10.270
In the suit, Crosby demanded that the city

00:10:10.270 --> 00:10:12.490
and the arresting officers
pay at least $50,000

00:10:12.490 --> 00:10:14.240
for compensatory and
punitive damages, fees,

00:10:14.240 --> 00:10:15.810
costs, and such other relief.

00:10:15.810 --> 00:10:18.810
In January 2019, the city
council voted eight to zero

00:10:18.810 --> 00:10:22.530
to settle with now Dr.
Crosby for $1.2 million.

00:10:22.530 --> 00:10:24.960
After the settlement, Dr. Crosby
stated that he was working

00:10:24.960 --> 00:10:26.270
on mending his reputation,

00:10:26.270 --> 00:10:28.530
having found himself often
defending his actions

00:10:28.530 --> 00:10:30.610
and explaining what happened that night

00:10:30.610 --> 00:10:32.620
once the case became a
now original headline.

00:10:32.620 --> 00:10:33.453
And it makes sense.

00:10:33.453 --> 00:10:34.360
If you just read the headlines,

00:10:34.360 --> 00:10:36.145
one could have gotten the
wrong idea about what happened,

00:10:36.145 --> 00:10:39.210
but moving forward, Dr. Crosby
said that his first steps

00:10:39.210 --> 00:10:41.890
post settlement would be
holding an implicit bias forum

00:10:41.890 --> 00:10:44.000
for students and faculty
at Stanford University

00:10:44.000 --> 00:10:45.970
where he earned his undergraduate degree.

00:10:45.970 --> 00:10:46.970
Okay, fine.

00:10:46.970 --> 00:10:48.950
You've probably heard about
police brutality before,

00:10:48.950 --> 00:10:50.920
so that one might not be that surprising,

00:10:50.920 --> 00:10:52.950
but have you heard the one about the aunt

00:10:52.950 --> 00:10:56.930
who sues her 12 year
old nephew for $127,000,

00:10:56.930 --> 00:10:58.270
because he hugged her?

00:10:58.270 --> 00:11:00.420
Well, there's an old saying
that when it comes to family,

00:11:00.420 --> 00:11:01.600
blood is thicker than water,

00:11:01.600 --> 00:11:03.420
but in the rough and
tumble world of litigation,

00:11:03.420 --> 00:11:05.180
it's not that uncommon
for aggrieved relatives

00:11:05.180 --> 00:11:06.870
to sue one another for personal injury.

00:11:06.870 --> 00:11:09.940
But what is uncommon is when
the plaintiff is an adult

00:11:09.940 --> 00:11:12.090
suing a very small child.

00:11:12.090 --> 00:11:14.010
And so goes to the story
of Jennifer Connell,

00:11:14.010 --> 00:11:17.020
the woman dubbed the aunt
from hell, who sued her nephew

00:11:17.020 --> 00:11:20.260
for $127,000 over a
birthday hug gone wrong.

00:11:20.260 --> 00:11:21.850
You probably heard some
of the facts of this case

00:11:21.850 --> 00:11:23.500
when the story first went viral.

00:11:23.500 --> 00:11:27.110
In 2011 an Upper East Side
aunt attended a birthday party

00:11:27.110 --> 00:11:29.010
for her then eight year old nephew, Sean.

00:11:29.010 --> 00:11:31.110
The precocious lad was riding around

00:11:31.110 --> 00:11:33.300
on his brand new bicycle,
his first two wheeler,

00:11:33.300 --> 00:11:34.780
when he saw his beloved aunt.

00:11:34.780 --> 00:11:35.940
And when he spotted her,

00:11:35.940 --> 00:11:37.810
Sean, who was technically
the son of a cousin,

00:11:37.810 --> 00:11:40.820
but refers to Connell as his
aunt, dropped his new bike

00:11:40.820 --> 00:11:43.130
on the ground and ran to
his aunt at full speed.

00:11:43.130 --> 00:11:46.860
He leapt into her arms exclaiming,
"Auntie Jen, I love you."

00:11:46.860 --> 00:11:49.120
Auntie Jen was caught off
guard by the leaping nephew,

00:11:49.120 --> 00:11:50.940
causing her to fall and break her wrist.

00:11:50.940 --> 00:11:54.470
Two years later, she sued young
Sean for hundred $127,000.

00:11:54.470 --> 00:11:56.360
Now, if this sounds ridiculous,

00:11:56.360 --> 00:11:58.420
you would not be alone in thinking so.

00:11:58.420 --> 00:11:59.510
When word of the case broke out,

00:11:59.510 --> 00:12:02.550
people blasted the aunt who
was a human resources manager

00:12:02.550 --> 00:12:05.580
on social media, dubbing
her the aunt from hell

00:12:05.580 --> 00:12:08.650
and the worst aunt
ever, and Auntie Christ.

00:12:08.650 --> 00:12:10.010
And the news media also piled on,

00:12:10.010 --> 00:12:11.830
portraying her as a greedy money grubber,

00:12:11.830 --> 00:12:14.560
bringing an inherently
ridiculous case against a child.

00:12:14.560 --> 00:12:15.850
The trial didn't help her image.

00:12:15.850 --> 00:12:19.080
Auntie Jen testified that her
injury harmed her social life

00:12:19.080 --> 00:12:21.530
by making her unable to hold
the plate of hors d'oeuvres.

00:12:21.530 --> 00:12:22.636
She further testified, quote,

00:12:22.636 --> 00:12:25.157
"I live in Manhattan in
a third-floor walk-up,

00:12:25.157 --> 00:12:26.207
"so it's been very difficult.

00:12:26.207 --> 00:12:28.480
"And we all know how
crowded it is in Manhattan."

00:12:28.480 --> 00:12:30.490
She argued that young
Sean had been negligent

00:12:30.490 --> 00:12:32.330
when he jumped into her
arms and broke her wrist,

00:12:32.330 --> 00:12:35.080
and, therefore, that she
was entitled to $127,000

00:12:35.080 --> 00:12:36.900
as compensation for her medical bills.

00:12:36.900 --> 00:12:38.350
Now, the jury disagreed deciding

00:12:38.350 --> 00:12:40.060
that she deserved zero compensation

00:12:40.060 --> 00:12:42.050
after a mere 20 minutes of deliberation.

00:12:42.050 --> 00:12:43.880
Now this seems like a textbook case

00:12:43.880 --> 00:12:45.270
of overzealous litigation.

00:12:45.270 --> 00:12:47.120
Well, here's the rub, and hold on,

00:12:47.120 --> 00:12:50.410
because it involves both legal
nuance and insurance law.

00:12:50.410 --> 00:12:52.540
I know everyone keeps asking
for more insurance law

00:12:52.540 --> 00:12:54.120
and extremely nuanced legal points,

00:12:54.120 --> 00:12:56.030
so, you know, here you go.

00:12:56.030 --> 00:12:58.570
Under Connecticut law, she
literally had no choice.

00:12:58.570 --> 00:13:00.020
Auntie Jen first submitted her claim

00:13:00.020 --> 00:13:02.550
to the homeowners liability
insurer of Sean's parents.

00:13:02.550 --> 00:13:03.940
In response, the insurer offered her

00:13:03.940 --> 00:13:05.640
a mere one dollar settlement offer.

00:13:05.640 --> 00:13:08.300
And when Auntie Jen wanted to
litigate her damages claim,

00:13:08.300 --> 00:13:10.450
her attorney's informed
her of an inconvenient fact

00:13:10.450 --> 00:13:11.680
about Connecticut law.

00:13:11.680 --> 00:13:14.140
It's illegal to name a
homeowner's insurance policy

00:13:14.140 --> 00:13:16.420
as a defendant in a personal injury case.

00:13:16.420 --> 00:13:18.820
The only way she could get a
homeowner's insurance policy

00:13:18.820 --> 00:13:21.300
to pay her medical bills
was to get a judgment

00:13:21.300 --> 00:13:22.930
against the person who injured her.

00:13:22.930 --> 00:13:25.490
So, regardless of how
silly it might've appeared

00:13:25.490 --> 00:13:26.810
to outside observers,

00:13:26.810 --> 00:13:29.320
Auntie Jen was required as a matter of law

00:13:29.320 --> 00:13:31.460
to sue the party that
she thought injured her,

00:13:31.460 --> 00:13:34.180
which in this case means
suing a young child.

00:13:34.180 --> 00:13:35.450
Auntie Jen did not win her case,

00:13:35.450 --> 00:13:37.760
but just because a party
loses a lawsuit doesn't mean

00:13:37.760 --> 00:13:39.780
that it was filed without any legal basis.

00:13:39.780 --> 00:13:42.260
A minor can be held liable
for injuring another

00:13:42.260 --> 00:13:43.320
under Connecticut law.

00:13:43.320 --> 00:13:46.140
In 1960, the Connecticut
Supreme Court had held

00:13:46.140 --> 00:13:48.200
that a 17 year old girl
could be held liable

00:13:48.200 --> 00:13:50.910
to her minor siblings
arising out of her negligence

00:13:50.910 --> 00:13:51.930
in a car accident.

00:13:51.930 --> 00:13:53.940
And an outlining the legal
standard of care for minors

00:13:53.940 --> 00:13:55.270
who injure third parties,

00:13:55.270 --> 00:13:56.527
the Connecticut Supreme Court wrote,

00:13:56.527 --> 00:13:59.057
"A minor is liable for
injuries negligently inflicted

00:13:59.057 --> 00:14:00.707
"by him upon another.

00:14:00.707 --> 00:14:03.077
"It is true that in determining
the negligence of a minor,

00:14:03.077 --> 00:14:05.157
"the law applies to him
a standard of conduct,

00:14:05.157 --> 00:14:06.757
"which will vary according to his age,

00:14:06.757 --> 00:14:08.077
"judgment and experience,

00:14:08.077 --> 00:14:10.407
"but the law does not grant
him a complete immunity

00:14:10.407 --> 00:14:13.980
"from liability for his
torts, even in negligence."

00:14:13.980 --> 00:14:16.630
So this lowered standard of
care for minors is consistent

00:14:16.630 --> 00:14:18.670
with what happened in Auntie Jen's case.

00:14:18.670 --> 00:14:20.470
At trial, the judge instructed the jury

00:14:20.470 --> 00:14:23.100
that had Sean been 18 at
the time of the incident,

00:14:23.100 --> 00:14:24.290
he probably would have been expected

00:14:24.290 --> 00:14:25.750
to act like a reasonable adult.

00:14:25.750 --> 00:14:28.208
However, because the boy
was only eight years old

00:14:28.208 --> 00:14:29.450
at the time, the jury had to consider

00:14:29.450 --> 00:14:32.160
only what a prudent eight
year old boy would have done.

00:14:32.160 --> 00:14:34.490
And on that basis, the
jury held that young Sean

00:14:34.490 --> 00:14:36.180
was not liable for any damages.

00:14:36.180 --> 00:14:38.840
After the case, Ms. Connell
appeared on the Today Show

00:14:38.840 --> 00:14:41.340
with her then twelve year
old nephew to clear the air.

00:14:41.340 --> 00:14:43.620
She explained that her
relationship remained sound

00:14:43.620 --> 00:14:46.200
and she deeply regretted that
Connecticut law forced her

00:14:46.200 --> 00:14:47.230
to take this route.

00:14:47.230 --> 00:14:49.940
- I was informed that
Sean had to be named.

00:14:49.940 --> 00:14:51.750
I was never comfortable with that.

00:14:51.750 --> 00:14:54.070
- As for young Sean,
he stands by his aunt.

00:14:54.070 --> 00:14:56.966
- Sean, what do you want
people to know about your aunt?

00:14:56.966 --> 00:15:00.140
- That I love her and she loves me.

00:15:00.140 --> 00:15:01.920
- Either way, this will probably make

00:15:01.920 --> 00:15:04.600
